        "tags": "Hold the lead voice exactly as uploaded throughout. Quilled baroque harpsichord and gut-strung concert harp carrying the melody over French touch house at 118 BPM. Eighteenth century French court keyboard writing meeting Supernature era French disco funk. Sidechained four on the floor kick, deep round analogue bass with an upright double bass walking above it, crisp closed hi-hats, single-coil wah guitar scratching sixteenths, a filtered disco loop with one long resonant sweep, close chamber strings. Discovery and Homework era styling, Music Sounds Better With You filter house warmth, Sacrebleu era lounge glamour. Vinyl crackle and tape saturation under everything, analogue console glue, wide stereo field, plate reverb on the choir. Grown MALE adult voices only.  Bilingual English verses and French refrain. Late night Paris rooftop, moonlit, elegant and melancholy.",
        "model": "v5.5",
        "created_at": "2026-08-25T13:42:39.590Z",
        "prompt": "[every section sung once only, adult voices throughout, lead voice forward and conversational, crisp storyteller diction, a quilled harpsichord and a gut-strung harp carry the melody in every section, all group lines carried by a mixed choir of grown men and women]\n\n[Opening Verse]\n[a quilled harpsichord picks out the tune, lead voice dry and close above it, one soft filtered pulse underneath]\nThere's a dog with his ear in a polished horn,\nAnd a lamb in a lab on a New Jersey morn,\nAnd we hand them the laurel, we always do,\nBut the first voice on record was neither of the two.\n\n[Verse]\n[same voice a shade brighter, deep analogue bass arrives under the harpsichord]\nIn eighteen and sixty a printer in France\nSang into a funnel and watched the line dance.\nNo speaker, no needle to give it all back.\nHe thought one day we'd learn reading in ripples and black.\n\n[Verse]\n[fuller now, handclaps on the backbeat, gut-strung harp arpeggios spilling across the bar]\nHe lodged it with science in sixty one,\nThey shelved it, they thanked him, and that was done.\nA drawing of singing. A silence to frame.\nA voice with no volume. A hearth with no flame.\n\n[Lead-in]\n[lead voice steps up, filter opening across the four lines, harpsichord doubling the vocal in octaves]\n\"Lend me your pen,\" says the song in the tune,\nAnd he lent it a pen in the light of the moon.\nIt wrote what it heard and it heard what it wrote,\nBut it couldn't read back a single note.\n\n[Refrain]\n[a massed choir of grown men and women joins full-throated, deep adult tone, cathedral weight, harp arpeggios rippling underneath, French lowercase and legato]\nau clair de la lune,\nprête-moi ta plume,\nma chandelle est morte,\nje n'ai plus de feu.\n\n[Verse]\n[choir drops away, lead voice alone, upright double bass walking forward, do NOT  sing fast, sing proper speed]\nThen a lab with a scanner, and a century plus,\nMade the soot turn to sound and the sound came to us.\nUp came a voice from the powder and pitch,\nA girl, and the whole world lost breath at the switch.\n\n[Verse]\n[building, close chamber strings in the upper register, harp climbing with them]\nThey christened her daughter. They gave her a name.\nThe wires ran the story, the whole planet came.\nTen seconds of girlhood in bottled-up black,\nThe very first voice, and we'd brought her back.\n\n[Refrain]\n[the massed choir of grown men and women returns full-throated, deep adult tone, hands high, harp and harpsichord together]\nau clair de la lune,\nprête-moi ta plume,\nma chandelle est morte,\nje n'ai plus de feu.\n\n[Bridge]\n[everything strips to one voice over a single sustained low note, harpsichord answering in bare single plucks, the room still]\nBut somebody checked what the tuning fork said,\nAnd the number was doubled. So halve it instead.\nAnd down went the pitch and out went the pace,\nAnd the girl became twenty long seconds of bass.\n\n[Breakdown]\n[the low note descends under the vocal, harp entering like water on the final two lines]\nIt was him all along, singing under his breath,\nAnd she went, in a year, from her birth to her death.\nShe was never a person. She never drew air.\nShe's an error in speed, and we put her there.\n\n[Instrumental]\n[French funk break for sixteen bars, wah guitar scratching sixteenths, popped upright bass, tight drums, harpsichord riffing over the top, purely instrumental]\n\n[Verse]\n[lead voice returns generous and open, full arrangement, harp and chamber strings together]\nWe all end as carbon, as ash and as smoke.\nShe started as carbon, and briefly she spoke.\nThen they fixed the arithmetic, gentle and slow,\nAnd the only girl ever made of soot had to go.\n\n[Final Refrain]\n[the massed choir of grown men and women at full height, one pass only, then the arrangement falls away to harpsichord]\nau clair de la lune,\nprête-moi ta plume,\nma chandelle est morte,\nje n'ai plus de feu.\n[no riffing] \n\n[sad spoken admission]]\n[the groove has stopped, one voice alone, close and unhurried, one plucked harpsichord note beneath it]\nma chandelle est morte.\n[stop promptly]",
