        "prompt": "Verse 1:\nI was down in the trenches, counting crumbs for a meal,\nSame boots every day, every step hard to feel.\nCold hands, long nights, no money in sight,\nStill I kept moving, held my line, held tight.\nEven when the fridge hummed loud and stayed bare,\nI learned how to stretch what was never really there.\nHead down, shoes scuffed, but I made my way through,\nOne foot in front of the other, doing what I had to do.\nI was clocking every corner, every coin in the floor,\nTrying not to let the panic kick the door.\nGot my name in the dust, got my back to the wall,\nStill I showed up early, still I answered the call.\n\nVerse 2:\nI watched folks around me carry more than their share,\nSeen their shoulders slump, the worry in their stare.\nSo when I got a little, I made sure I was there,\nNo questions asked, just ready to repair.\nPaid rent for my brother, got heat for my mom,\nKept the lights on steady, chased away the storm.\nPut tools in my people’s hands when the road got long,\nShowed 'em how to build it, made their spirits strong.\nIf somebody was short, I’d slide in with the fix,\nIf the cupboard was empty, I’d show up with the bricks.\nI learned what it meant when you don’t get to fall,\nYou hold somebody up, or you don’t stand tall.\n\nVerse 3:\nNow the checks hit direct, same Friday, same hour,\nNo more scraping change just to keep on the power.\nI still think about that Greenpoint basement floor,\nCardboard by the oven, that draft under the door.\nThe Super used to shrug when the sink ran slow,\nAnd the freezer made that rattle like it wouldn't let go.\nSo I send my brother cash before he even asks,\nPay the ConEd bill, then I fill up the gas.\nI cover missed school pictures, the sneakers, the rent,\nAnd make sure the people who held me up get what I meant.\nNow I’m checking off the list before the sun gets low,\nTexting out the help, making sure the whole house knows.\nEvery dollar got a job, every week got a plan,\nAnd I move like somebody who remembers where I began.\n\nVerse 4:\nWe eat good now, no need to count every plate,\nSteaks on the grill, no more staring at the gate.\nKids got clean clothes, new shoes for every race,\nAnd the bills don’t wait, they're paid right in their place.\nFrom the trenches to the table, we came out alive,\nThrough every struggle, we learned how to thrive.\nBuilt a whole new life, where our dreams can arrive,\nAnd we all get to shine, every single one of us survives.\nI still remember when a full plate felt like luck,\nNow the table stays crowded, and nobody’s stuck.\nLaughter in the kitchen, plates sliding around,\nReal heat in the rooms, real love all around.",
