    "prompt": "Verse 1\nWhite ribbon holding up a midnight sky\nRiding in the backseat while the pines drift by\nEverybody says she’s pretty when she doesn’t speak\nLike silence is a language that belongs to me\n\nWindow catching ghosts in the afternoon\nSummer hangs around like cheap perfume\nGot a whole constellation hiding underneath my skin\nNo one’s looking close enough to let me in\n\nMama says, “Don’t look so sad…”\nBut sadness wears expensive black\nAnd I’ve been practicing forever\nHow to disappear without leaving\n\nI was born with cinema inside my veins\nDriving through America beneath the rain\nEvery town’s another promise that forgets my name\nStill I wear it like a diamond anyway\n\nIf somebody loved me, they were always passing through\nLike highway lights that only teach the dark what not to do\nI keep the windows open though the cold comes rushing in\nMaybe something beautiful knows where I’ve been\n\nLater on my hair fell down around my shoulders\nEveryone got older, I just learned composure\nPlaid shirt, little gold around my neck\nTrying to look fearless with a heart that wouldn’t rest\n\nCounting all the cracks inside the ceiling paint\nDreaming of the City from another state\nIf freedom has a face, I swear I’ve seen her once\nShe looked back at me before she disappeared\n\nMaybe I belong to all the roads I never chose\nTo diners after midnight and forgotten Broadway shows\nTo lipstick on a cigarette\nTo prayers I never finished\nTo girls who grow up too fast\nWho spend forever trying to bring them back \n\n\nI was born with cinema inside my veins\nMaking saints from ordinary Saturdays\nLearning every beautiful thing eventually fades\nBut somehow leaves its color anyway\n\nSo if you see me driving with the windows rolled back\nDon’t ask me where I’m going—I was never good at that\nI’m just following a feeling older than my name\nTrying to find the girl who looked away…\n\n…before the world could make her stay\n\n….with a cinema inside my veins",
